\section*{Condensation in every dilute hard-sphere ground state}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Every ground state of a sufficiently dilute fixed-density three-dimensional hard-sphere gas is claimed to place a uniform positive particle fraction in the constant orbital.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} For the three-dimensional torus hard-sphere Bose Hamiltonian, impose Dirichlet exclusion at pair distance \(a\), with kinetic energy \(-\sum_i\Delta_i\). For a normalized symmetric ground vector \(\Psi\), put
\[
 B(\Psi)=N^{-1}\langle L^{-3/2},\gamma^{(1)}_\Psi L^{-3/2}\rangle,
 \qquad\operatorname{Tr}\gamma^{(1)}_\Psi=N.
\]
The claimed universal constants \(\varepsilon_0,c_0>0\) give
\[
 \rho a^3<\varepsilon_0,\quad N_k,L_k\to\infty,\quad N_k/L_k^3\to\rho
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad\liminf_k B(\Psi_k)\ge c_0
\]
for every choice of ground vectors, allowing complex vectors and degeneracy.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Energy and spatial bounds produce stationary hard-core path samples. Neighbor-cell moves, retained movable groups, and routes through sparse obstacles compare phase coherence across the volume, giving a constant-mode lower bound also for complex ground states.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Every ground state of a sufficiently dilute fixed-density three-dimensional hard-sphere gas is claimed to place a uniform positive particle fraction in the constant orbital. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 267, the selected statement is a density-uniform hard-sphere ground-state condensate lower bound for pure and mixed ground states. Positive-temperature condensation and quantum-depletion asymptotics are beyond it. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{HardSphere.lean}. The inventory does not supply a named declaration for these Comparator entries. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

